Part A - Basic grep and Filtering


•	Display all lines containing the word failed.
grep "failed" security_log.txt

•	Display all lines containing failed, ignoring case.
grep -i "failed" security_log.txt

•	Display all lines that contain either Failed or Denied.
grep -E "Failed|Denied" security_log.txt

•	Display all lines that do not contain the word INFO.
grep -v "INFO" security_log.txt

 
Display all lines containing the exact word root.
grep -w "root" security_log.txt

•	Display all occurrences of the word attack along with their line numbers.
grep -n "attack" security_log.txt

•	Count the number of lines containing ERROR.
grep -c "ERROR" security_log.txt

•	Display only the filenames of files whose contents contain the word malware, assuming multiple log files are available.
grep -l "malware" *.txt

•	Display the first five lines containing Suspicious.
grep "Suspicious" security_log.txt | head -5

•	Display the last five failed-login entries.
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | tail -5

 
Part B - Regular Expression Extraction


•	Extract all IPv4 addresses from the log.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt

•	Extract all IP addresses belonging to the 10.x.x.x private network.
grep -Eo '\b10\.([0-9]{1,3}\.){2}[0-9]{1,3}\b' security_log.txt

•	Extract all addresses belonging to the 192.168.x.x network.
grep -Eo '\b192\.168\.([0-9]{1,3}\.)[0-9]{1,3}\b' security_log.txt

 
•	Extract all email addresses.
grep -Eo '[[:alnum:]._%+-]+@[[:alnum:].-]+\.[[:alpha:]]+' security_log.txt

•	Extract only email addresses whose domain ends in .com.
grep -Eo '[[:alnum:]._%+-]+@[[:alnum:].-]+\.com\b' security_log.txt

•	Extract email addresses whose username contains at least one digit.
grep -Eo '[[:alnum:]._%+-]*[0-9][[:alnum:]._%+-]*@[[:alnum:].-]+\.[[:alpha:]]+' security_log.txt

•	Extract all URLs beginning with http:// or https://.
grep -Eo 'https?://[^[:space:]]+' security_log.txt

•	Extract only HTTPS URLs.
grep -Eo 'https://[^[:space:]]+' security_log.txt

 
•	Extract URLs ending with .php.
grep -Eo 'https?://[^[:space:]]+\.php\b' security_log.txt

•	Extract all CVE identifiers in the form:CVE-YYYY-NNNN
grep -Eo 'CVE-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4,}' security_log.txt

•	Extract only CVEs reported for the year 2025.
grep -Eo 'CVE-2025-[0-9]{4,}' security_log.txt

•	Extract all 32-character hexadecimal MD5 hashes.
grep -Eo '\b[[:xdigit:]]{32}\b' security_log.txt

•	Extract all 40-character hexadecimal SHA-1 hashes.
grep -Eo '\b[[:xdigit:]]{40}\b' security_log.txt

•	Extract all 64-character hexadecimal SHA-256 hashes.
grep -Eo '\b[[:xdigit:]]{64}\b' security_log.txt

 
•	Extract all timestamps having the format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
grep -Eo '[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}' security_log.txt



Part C - Anchors and Regex Patterns
•	Display all lines beginning with a timestamp.
grep -E '^\[[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}\]' security_log.txt

•	Display all lines beginning with the word Suspicious.
grep '^Suspicious' security_log.txt

•	Display lines ending with a numeric value.
grep -E '[0-9]+$' security_log.txt

 
•	Display lines beginning with ERROR or WARNING.
grep -E '^(ERROR|WARNING)' security_log.txt

•	Display lines whose first character is a digit.
grep -E '^[0-9]' security_log.txt

•	Find lines ending in .exe.
grep -E '\.exe$' security_log.txt

•	Find lines containing filenames ending in .exe, .sh, or .pdf.
grep -E '[[:alnum:]_.-]+\.(exe|sh|pdf)\b' security_log.txt

•	Identify lines containing the exact word root, but not words such as rooted.
grep -w "root" security_log.txt

•	Display blank lines in the file.
grep '^$' security_log.txt

•	Display all nonblank lines.
grep -v '^$' security_log.txt

 
Part D - Linux Filters and Pipelines
•	Extract all IPv4 addresses, sort them, and remove duplicates.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort -V | uniq

•	Count the number of unique IPv4 addresses present in the log.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort -V | uniq | wc -l

•	Extract all IP addresses and display them in sorted order.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort -V

•	Determine which IP addresses appear more than once.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort -V | uniq -c | awk '$1 > 1'

•	Determine the IP address that occurs most frequently.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -1

•	Extract all usernames from authentication entries and display unique usernames.
grep -i "login for user" security_log.txt | grep -Eo 'user [[:alnum:]_]+' | awk '{print $2}' | sort -u

 
•	Count the number of authentication attempts associated with each username
grep -i "login for user" security_log.txt | grep -Eo 'user [[:alnum:]_]+' | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq
-c

•	Extract all HTTP status codes and determine how many times each status code occurs.
grep -Eo 'status [0-9]{3}' security_log.txt | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq -c

•	Display the five most frequently occurring IP addresses.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -5

•	Display the three most frequently occurring usernames.
grep -i "login for user" security_log.txt | grep -Eo 'user [[:alnum:]_]+' | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq
-c | sort -nr | head -3
•	Extract all destination ports and display them in numerical order.
grep -Eo 'port [0-9]+' security_log.txt | awk '{print $2}' | sort -n

•	Remove duplicate port numbers.
grep -Eo 'port [0-9]+' security_log.txt | awk '{print $2}' | sort -n | uniq
 
•	Count the number of unique destination ports.
grep -Eo 'port [0-9]+' security_log.txt | awk '{print $2}' | sort -n | uniq | wc -l

•	Extract all file extensions appearing in the log and determine their frequency.
grep -Eo '\.[[:alnum:]]+' security_log.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

•	Find the five most frequently occurring domains.
grep -Eo '\b([[:alnum:]-]+\.)+[[:alpha:]]{2,}\b' security_log.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -5



Part E - Combining grep Conditions
•	Display lines containing both an IP address and the word Failed.
grep -E 'Failed' security_log.txt | grep -E '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}'

•	Display lines containing both root and Failed.
grep -i "Failed" security_log.txt | grep -w "root"

•	Display lines containing either download or upload.
grep -Ei 'download|upload' security_log.txt
 
•	Display suspicious entries containing either .exe or .sh.
grep "Suspicious" security_log.txt | grep -E '\.(exe|sh)\b'

•	Display lines containing a CVE identifier and an IP address.
grep -E 'CVE-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4,}' security_log.txt | grep -E '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}'

•	Display lines containing both a filename and a hash value.
grep -E '\.(exe|sh|pdf)\b' security_log.txt | grep -Ei 'MD5=|SHA1|SHA256'

•	Display entries containing an IP address followed by a port.
grep -E '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}:[0-9]+|([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}.*port [0-9]+'
security_log.txt

•	Display HTTP requests containing both a URL and status code 404.
grep -E 'https?://.*status 404' security_log.txt

•	Display authentication failures but exclude entries generated from 127.0.0.1.
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | grep -v "127\.0\.0\.1"

 
•	Display attack-related entries but exclude informational (INFO) messages.
grep -Ei 'attack|exploit|malware|suspicious' security_log.txt | grep -v "INFO"



Part F - Negative Matching
•	Extract all IPv4 addresses except those beginning with 127..
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | grep -v '^127\.'

•	Display all log entries except successful login events.
grep -Eiv 'successful login' security_log.txt

•	Display all entries that do not contain INFO, DEBUG, or SUCCESS.
grep -Ev 'INFO|DEBUG|SUCCESS' security_log.txt

•	Find HTTP requests whose status code is not 200.
grep -E 'https?://' security_log.txt | grep -Ev 'status 200'

 
•	Display suspicious traffic excluding connections to 192.168.x.x.
grep "Suspicious" security_log.txt | grep -Ev '192\.168\.'

•	Extract email addresses except those belonging to the organization's domain.
grep -Eo '[[:alnum:]._%+-]+@[[:alnum:].-]+\.[[:alpha:]]+' security_log.txt | grep -v '@example\.com$'



Part G - Redirection
•	Extract all IPv4 addresses and save them into:
ips.txt
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt > ips.txt


•	Extract all email addresses and save them into:
emails.txt
grep -Eo '[[:alnum:]._%+-]+@[[:alnum:].-]+\.[[:alpha:]]+' security_log.txt > emails.txt


•	Extract all URLs and save them into:
urls.txt
grep -Eo 'https?://[^[:space:]]+' security_log.txt > urls.txt


•	Extract all hashes and save them into:
hashes.txt
grep -Eio '\b[[:xdigit:]]{32}|\b[[:xdigit:]]{40}|\b[[:xdigit:]]{64}' security_log.txt > hashes.txt


•	Extract failed-login entries and save them into: failed_login.txt
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt > failed_login.txt
 
•	Extract suspicious entries and append them to:
incident_report.txt
grep "Suspicious" security_log.txt >> incident_report.txt


•	Store unique IP addresses in:
unique_ips.txt
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort -V | uniq > unique_ips.txt


•	Store IP-frequency statistics in:
ip_frequency.txt
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr > ip_frequency.txt


•	Redirect normal output to results.txt and errors to errors.txt.
grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' security_log.txt > results.txt 2> errors.txt


•	Extract all domains from the log entries containing Domain accessed, convert the domain names to lowercase, sort them alphabetically, remove duplicates, and save the result in domains.txt.
grep "Domain accessed" security_log.txt | grep -Eo '([[:alnum:]-]+\.)+[[:alpha:]]{2,}' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | sort -u > domains.txt


•	identify all IP addresses involved in failed-login attempts. Convert the output into a clean list containing one unique IP per line and save it as failed_login_ips.txt.
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' | sort -V -u > failed_login_ips.txt


Combining filters
•	Extract all failed-login usernames and save them into:
failed_users.txt
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | grep -Eo 'user [[:alnum:]_]+' | awk '{print $2}' > failed_users.txt


•	Extract all domains and save them in lowercase into:
domains.txt
