•	Extract all MD5 hashes, convert them to uppercase, and save them into: md5_hashes.txt
grep -Eo '[[:xdigit:]]{32}' security_log.txt | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]' > md5_hashes.txt


•	Extract source IP addresses from failed-login records, sort them, remove duplicates, and redirect the result to:
failed_ips.txt
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | grep -Eo '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' | sort -V -u > failed_ips.txt


•	Create a file named clean_log.txt in which all square brackets are removed from the original log.
sed 's/\[//g; s/\]//g' security_log.txt > clean_log.txt


•	Create normalized_log.txt by converting the complete log file to lowercase.
tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' < security_log.txt > normalized_log.txt


•	Extract all domain names, convert them to lowercase, sort them, remove duplicates, and save them to unique_domains.txt.
grep -Eo '([[:alnum:]-]+\.)+[[:alpha:]]{2,}' security_log.txt | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | sort -u > unique_domains.txt


•	Find all failed-login entries and extract only the usernames.
grep -i "Failed login" security_log.txt | grep -Eo 'user [[:alnum:]_]+' | awk '{print $2}'

•	Find all failed-login entries, extract only the source IP addresses, and display them in sorted order.
